Z||000000||Turbo88||10-20-2001||11:18 PM||||I finally found a sticking point. To make a long story short, my fuel pump is not pumping fuel. I found that the relay was bad so I replaced that. No dice. Where I am confused is that I have found some conflicting information relating to the oil pressure sending switch. I did I'm seeing oil pressure on my gauge but will the car start and run without the OP switch connected to the harness (just curious). Also, what else should I check before I drop the tank? I do have a new fuel pump but I'm trying to get some good solid work done on the turbo kit so I'm lazy to all the other problems. Here is some history on the car that might relate...<P>The car has been sitting for about 3 1/2 year but was started about a year ago. It had less than a 1/4 tank which I've put 5 more gallons of fresh fuel in. <P>Another question I have is; how load are the stock fuel pumps? Should I beable to hear it from outside the car? Next to the tank? I know some cars are different and I've heard both ways on Fiero's. <P>You guys will have to forgive my Fiero ignorance here but understand I'm starting with a dead car, not a running one!<P>Thanks for any help<BR>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||10-21-2001||03:11 AM||||check the 5 amp fuse under the dash in the fuse block (second from the left on the top row) and make sure it is good. Also, did you replace the relay with a new one or a used one? You might try another one. Then you can also check the wiring connection from the pump (on the firewall in the middle under the back window).<P>You will have oil pressure regardless of whether the sending unit is connected or not.  The fuel pump should be heard when turning on the ignition (before cranking the starter).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Turbo88||10-21-2001||08:52 AM||||The fuse was the first thing I checked (a little bit of wishful thinking on my part? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. On my car it is a 10 amp fuse though. I checked the wiring in the hopes that I just forgot to plug something in when I reinstalled the cradle. The relay is a brand new unit. <P>I know I'll have oil pressure reguardless but I had read that there is an  override built into the code on the ecm that will send power to the fuel pumnp if it detected oil pressure. I was wondering if that override would cause the fuel pump not to run if the ecm does not see oil pressure. <P>Book says the fuel pump will run for 2 seconds when the key is turned into the on position then will shut off until the engine is started. It has a new fuel filter on it as well. Out of curiousity, I disconnected the fuel lines where the cross connects near the battery are to see if there was any fuel in the line.. there was none. At this point, I am going to pull the filter off and see if there is pressure there, recheck wires and prepare to pull a nearly full gas tank! Maybe it was good I started this project with a dead car, I'm learning it's demons before I start modding it..<P>Thanks TGowens<BR>-Bryan||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnt671||10-21-2001||09:40 AM||||The way the override works is the fuel pump will not pump until the oil pressure reaches a certain point.  The engine will crank until the oil pressure light goes out.  If you aren't sending oil pressure info to the ECM the pump won't start in the override mode.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-21-2001||11:57 AM||||Are you sure that you didn't miss a ground wire during the install?<P>Or forget to plug in the plug? That one is easy to miss.<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Turbo88||10-21-2001||07:05 PM||||It was the fuel pump. Indy_guy, I know what you mean about that little connector but for some reason through out this whole deal that connector is always staring me in the face!!! In any case, it was the fuel pump. The tank is easy to remove accept for those damn hoses. I'm going to post my story about the clutch later. Dinner is calling!!<P>Thanks for the assistence,<BR>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
